    "redis-command-generator": {
      name: "Redis Command Builder & Key Helper",
      metadataTitle: "Redis Command Builder & Key Helper Online",
      description: "Build Redis CLI commands for hashes, sets, sorted sets, lists, and expiration TTL.",
      longDescription: "Build valid Redis CLI commands for Strings, Hashes, Lists, Sets, Sorted Sets (ZSET), and key TTL expiration—plus non-blocking SCAN patterns and atomic SET NX EX lock commands.",
      keywords: [
        "redis command generator",
        "redis cli builder",
        "redis set nx ex lock",
        "redis hset zadd command",
        "redis ttl expire scan",
        "redis key helper",
      ],
      faqs: [
        {
          question: "Why should I use SET key val NX EX 30 instead of running SETNX followed by EXPIRE?",
          answer: "Running SETNX and EXPIRE as two separate commands leaves a window where a client crash creates a lock key with no expiration forever. SET ... NX EX sets the value only if absent and attaches the TTL atomically.",
        },
        {
          question: "Why does calling SET overwrite or clear an existing key's TTL in Redis?",
          answer: "By design, SET replaces the key's metadata and clears any prior expiration timer. Pass KEEPTTL (or specify EX/PX again) if updating a key's value must preserve its remaining lifetime.",
        },
        {
          question: "Why is SCAN safer than KEYS pattern* in production Redis?",
          answer: "KEYS performs a full blocking O(N) sweep of the entire keyspace in one command, freezing all other queries. SCAN iterates incrementally in small batches via a cursor so other clients stay responsive.",
        },
      ],
      answerSections: [
        {
          heading: "Atomic Redis commands for caches, locks, and collections",
          paragraphs: [
            "Redis executes commands single-threadedly, so choosing atomic compound commands prevents race conditions between separate round-trips. For example, SET lock:job token NX EX 30 sets a distributed lock and its 30-second TTL in one atomic operation—avoiding orphaned locks if a client crashes between SETNX and EXPIRE.",
            "Using structured colon-separated key namespaces (such as user:42:sessions or ratelimit:ip:10.0.0.1) keeps keys easy to audit and target with pattern scans.",
          ],
          codeExamples: [
            {
              title: "Atomic Redis lock, hash update, and sorted-set leaderboard commands",
              language: "bash",
              code: "SET lock:billing:99 \"req-abc\" NX EX 30\nHSET session:u42 role \"admin\" last_seen \"2026-04-10T12:00:00Z\"\nZADD leaderboard:daily 1450 \"player:7\"",
            },
          ],
        },
        {
          heading: "Production-safety pitfalls: KEYS * vs. SCAN and TTL resets",
          paragraphs: [
            "Running the wrong inspection or mutation command on a busy Redis instance can stall all client connections.",
          ],
          bullets: [
            "Never run KEYS * on production Redis—KEYS blocks the entire single-threaded server while scanning millions of keys. Use cursor-based SCAN 0 MATCH prefix:* COUNT 100 instead.",
            "Calling plain SET key value on an existing key with a TTL removes its expiration and makes the key persistent unless you pass EX, PX, or KEEPTTL.",
            "Prefer UNLINK over DEL for large hashes, lists, or sets so Redis frees memory asynchronously in a background thread instead of blocking the event loop.",
            "Quote string values containing spaces or special characters when pasting into redis-cli.",
          ],
        },
      ],
      howToUseSteps: [
        "Choose a Redis data structure or operation (SET with EX/NX, HSET/HGETALL, ZADD/ZRANGE, LPUSH/LRANGE, or SCAN).",
        "Fill in the key namespace, field/member arguments, score or TTL seconds, and quoting options.",
        "Copy the generated redis-cli command and verify any production-safety warnings before executing.",
      ],
    },
    "systemd-unit-generator": {
      name: "Linux Systemd Service Unit Generator",
      metadataTitle: "Linux Systemd Service Unit Generator Online",
      description: "Generate systemd .service unit configuration files for Node.js, Python, and Go daemons.",
      longDescription: "Generate production-hardened Linux systemd .service unit files for Node.js, Python, and Go daemons with Restart=on-failure policies, EnvironmentFile loading, WorkingDirectory, and security sandboxing directives.",
      keywords: [
        "systemd unit generator",
        "systemd service file builder",
        "nodejs python go systemd service",
        "systemd restart on-failure",
        "linux daemon service unit",
        "systemd security hardening",
      ],
      faqs: [
        {
          question: "Why does my systemd service fail with status=203/EXEC even though the command works in bash?",
          answer: "Exit code 203/EXEC usually means ExecStart uses a relative binary name instead of an absolute path (like /usr/bin/node), references a shell builtin, lacks execute permissions (+x), or has an invalid shebang/CRLF line ending.",
        },
        {
          question: "What is the difference between Restart=on-failure and Restart=always in a .service file?",
          answer: "Restart=always restarts the process even when it exits cleanly with status 0 (making manual in-process shutdowns harder). Restart=on-failure restarts only on non-zero exit codes, uncaught signals, or watchdog timeouts.",
        },
        {
          question: "Can a systemd .service EnvironmentFile use shell export statements or variable expansion?",
          answer: "No. Systemd EnvironmentFile expects simple KEY=VALUE lines without 'export ' prefixes and does not evaluate shell commands or ${OTHER_VAR} substitutions inside the .env file.",
        },
      ],
      answerSections: [
        {
          heading: "Essential systemd .service sections: [Unit], [Service], and [Install]",
          paragraphs: [
            "A Linux systemd service unit coordinates startup dependencies ([Unit] After=network-online.target), process supervision ([Service] Type=simple, ExecStart, Restart=on-failure), and boot targets ([Install] WantedBy=multi-user.target). Modern Node.js, Python, and Go servers run in the foreground without forking, matching Type=simple (the default).",
            "Systemd captures stdout and stderr automatically into journald, so logs are inspected via journalctl -u myapp -f without custom logrotate scripts.",
          ],
          codeExamples: [
            {
              title: "Hardened Go/Node.js systemd .service unit file",
              language: "ini",
              code: "[Unit]\nDescription=API Daemon\nAfter=network-online.target\nWants=network-online.target\n\n[Service]\nType=simple\nUser=appuser\nWorkingDirectory=/opt/app\nEnvironmentFile=-/opt/app/.env\nExecStart=/usr/bin/node dist/server.js\nRestart=on-failure\nRestartSec=5s\nNoNewPrivileges=true\n\n[Install]\nWantedBy=multi-user.target",
            },
          ],
        },
        {
          heading: "ExecStart path rules, EnvironmentFile, and crash-loop protection",
          paragraphs: [
            "A handful of systemd unit mistakes prevent services from starting or trap them in infinite restart loops.",
          ],
          bullets: [
            "ExecStart must specify an absolute path to the binary (e.g., /usr/bin/node or /opt/venv/bin/uvicorn)—systemd does NOT expand $PATH or shell globs.",
            "Prefix EnvironmentFile=-/path/to/.env with a leading '-' if the file is optional so systemd does not fail unit startup when absent.",
            "Prefer Restart=on-failure over Restart=always if a clean exit code 0 should stay stopped; pair with StartLimitIntervalSec and StartLimitBurst in [Unit] to halt endless crash loops.",
            "Run systemd-analyze verify /etc/systemd/system/myapp.service after saving to catch syntax typos before starting.",
          ],
        },
      ],
      howToUseSteps: [
        "Select the runtime preset (Node.js, Python/Uvicorn, or Go binary) and enter the service name, ExecStart absolute path, and non-root User.",
        "Configure Restart policy, RestartSec backoff, EnvironmentFile path, and optional security hardening flags (NoNewPrivileges, ProtectSystem).",
        "Copy the .service unit into /etc/systemd/system/<name>.service and run systemctl daemon-reload && systemctl enable --now <name>.",
      ],
    },
