    "json-to-python-dataclass": {
      name: "JSON to Python Dataclass Converter",
      metadataTitle: "JSON to Python Dataclass Converter Online",
      description: "Convert JSON objects into Python 3.10+ @dataclass classes.",
      longDescription: "Generate Python 3.10+ `@dataclass` definitions from nested JSON objects and arrays. Infers PEP 604 `X | None` union annotations, nested dataclass trees, `field(default_factory=list)` defaults, and optional `slots=True` / `frozen=True` decorators.",
      keywords: [
        "json to python dataclass",
        "convert json to dataclass 3.10",
        "python dataclass generator from json",
        "nested json to python dataclasses",
        "pep 604 dataclass type hints",
        "json schema to python dataclass",
      ],
      faqs: [
        {
          question: "Why does Python dataclass require field(default_factory=list) instead of = []?",
          answer: "Python shares default argument objects across instances. To prevent all instances from sharing one mutable list, @dataclass raises ValueError on '= []' and requires field(default_factory=list).",
        },
        {
          question: "Does passing json.loads() dict to a dataclass automatically instantiate nested dataclasses?",
          answer: "No. Standard @dataclass(**raw_dict) leaves nested JSON objects as plain dicts. Use a helper like dacite.from_dict() or switch to Pydantic BaseModel for recursive hydration.",
        },
        {
          question: "What Python version is needed for list[str] and str | None annotations?",
          answer: "Built-in generic list[...] arrived in Python 3.9 and the X | None union operator (PEP 604) arrived in Python 3.10.",
        },
      ],
      answerSections: [
        {
          heading: "Type inference and mutable default safety in Python dataclasses",
          paragraphs: [
            "Converting nested JSON into Python 3.10+ `@dataclass` classes extracts each nested object into a dedicated class ordered bottom-up (child classes before parent classes) so type annotations resolve without `from __future__ import annotations` forward references.",
            "In Python dataclasses, assigning a mutable list or dict directly (`tags: list[str] = []`) raises a `ValueError: mutable default` at import time. Collection defaults must always wrap inside `field(default_factory=list)`.",
          ],
          bullets: [
            "JSON `null` values or keys missing across array items infer `T | None = None` using Python 3.10+ union syntax.",
            "Non-empty homogeneous arrays infer `list[str]`, `list[int]`, or `list[ChildItem]` rather than untyped `list[Any]`.",
            "Enabling `slots=True` reduces per-instance memory overhead in Python 3.10+ when instantiating thousands of records.",
          ],
          codeExamples: [
            {
              title: "Nested JSON converted to Python 3.10+ @dataclass",
              language: "python",
              code: "from dataclasses import dataclass, field\n\n@dataclass(slots=True)\nclass Author:\n    id: int\n    name: str\n\n@dataclass(slots=True)\nclass Article:\n    title: str\n    author: Author\n    tags: list[str] = field(default_factory=list)",
            },
          ],
        },
        {
          heading: "Deserialization caveats vs. Pydantic models",
          paragraphs: [
            "Standard library `@dataclass` constructors do not recursively hydrate nested dicts from `json.loads()` (`Article(**data)` leaves `author` as a raw `dict` unless converted with `dacite`, `mashumaro`, or a custom `__post_init__`).",
            "If you need runtime validation, camelCase-to-snake_case aliasing, or automatic recursive parsing of untrusted API JSON, prefer Pydantic V2 `BaseModel` over plain dataclasses.",
          ],
        },
      ],
      howToUseSteps: [
        "Paste a sample JSON object or array payload into the editor.",
        "Enter the root dataclass name and toggle Python 3.10+ `| None` unions, `frozen=True`, or `slots=True` options.",
        "Copy the generated `@dataclass` classes into your Python module.",
      ],
    },
    "json-to-go-struct": {
      name: "JSON to Go Struct Converter",
      metadataTitle: "JSON to Go Struct Converter Online",
      description: "Convert JSON objects into Go structs with json tags.",
      longDescription: "Convert JSON objects and API responses into idiomatic Go `struct` definitions complete with `json:\"key\"` struct tags. Handles Go PascalCase export capitalization, initialisms (`ID`, `URL`, `HTTP`), nested structs vs. inline structs, and `omitempty` / pointer nullability.",
      keywords: [
        "json to go struct",
        "convert json to golang struct",
        "go struct json tags generator",
        "json to go inline nested struct",
        "golang encoding json struct builder",
        "go omitempty struct generator",
      ],
      faqs: [
        {
          question: "Why must Go struct fields start with an uppercase letter when the JSON key is lowercase?",
          answer: "Go's encoding/json package ignores unexported (lowercase) fields during reflection. Capitalizing the Go field name (e.g., Email) while attaching `json:\"email\"` keeps the struct exported and preserves the lowercase JSON wire key.",
        },
        {
          question: "When should I use a pointer (*string, *bool) instead of a value type with omitempty?",
          answer: "Use pointers whenever you need to distinguish an omitted/null field from a valid zero value like 0, false, or an empty string—especially when sending PATCH updates.",
        },
        {
          question: "Does Go use interface{} or any for unknown JSON values?",
          answer: "Modern Go (1.18+) defines 'any' as a built-in alias for 'interface{}', so both compile identically across Go toolchains.",
        },
      ],
      answerSections: [
        {
          heading: "Go export rules, initialisms, and json struct tag semantics",
          paragraphs: [
            "Go's `encoding/json` package uses reflection and can only marshal or unmarshal exported struct fields starting with an uppercase letter. Converting JSON keys like `user_id` or `api_url` produces Go-idiomatic PascalCase fields (`UserID`, `APIURL`) paired with exact `json:\"user_id\"` struct tags so wire keys stay unchanged.",
            "Integer vs. float detection inspects JSON numbers across all array samples: whole numbers emit `int64` (or `int`), whereas decimals emit `float64`.",
          ],
          bullets: [
            "`json:\"field,omitempty\"` omits zero-value fields (`\"\"`, `0`, `false`, `nil`) when marshaling back to JSON.",
            "Nullable fields (`null`) or optional numbers where `0` is meaningful should use pointer types (`*int64`, `*string`) rather than bare values.",
            "Heterogeneous or arbitrary key-value objects fall back cleanly to `map[string]any` (Go 1.18+ alias for `interface{}`).",
          ],
          codeExamples: [
            {
              title: "JSON API response converted to idiomatic Go structs",
              language: "go",
              code: "type Order struct {\n\tOrderID   string  `json:\"order_id\"`\n\tAmountUSD float64 `json:\"amount_usd\"`\n\tPaid      bool    `json:\"paid\"`\n\tTags      []string `json:\"tags,omitempty\"`\n}",
            },
          ],
        },
        {
          heading: "Zero-value omitempty traps with booleans and numbers",
          paragraphs: [
            "Adding `,omitempty` to a non-pointer `bool` or `int` field causes `json.Marshal` to drop `false` and `0` completely—breaking APIs that distinguish missing fields from explicit `false` or `0`.",
            "In Go 1.24+, the new `omitzero` struct tag option works alongside pointers for cleaner optional-field serialization.",
          ],
        },
      ],
      howToUseSteps: [
        "Paste a valid JSON object or array payload into the editor.",
        "Set the root Go struct name and toggle inline nested structs vs. extracted top-level structs and `omitempty` tags.",
        "Copy the generated Go struct declarations directly into your package and unmarshal with `encoding/json`.",
      ],
    },
