      faqs: [
        {
          question: "What is the main difference between .gitignore and .dockerignore evaluation?",
          answer: ".dockerignore lives only at the build context root (unlike nested .gitignore files) and uses Go filepath.Match semantics with ** support, plus special handling where excluding a directory does not block explicit !dir/file negation rules.",
        },
        {
          question: "How do I debug which .gitignore file is ignoring a file in a real repository?",
          answer: "Run git check-ignore -v path/to/file in your terminal. Git prints the exact .gitignore file, line number, and rule pattern—including global core.excludesFile and .git/info/exclude.",
        },
        {
          question: "Why does a leading slash change how a pattern matches?",
          answer: "Without a slash, *.sqlite or temp matches in every subdirectory. Prefixing / (for example /temp) anchors the rule strictly to the directory containing that .gitignore file.",
        },
      ],
      answerSections: [
        {
          heading: "How Git evaluates .gitignore precedence and glob syntax",
          paragraphs: [
            "Git evaluates patterns sequentially from top to bottom within a .gitignore file, and the last matching pattern determines whether a path is ignored or tracked. A pattern without a slash (like *.log) matches at any directory depth, whereas a leading or middle slash (like /build or config/*.json) anchors the pattern relative to the .gitignore directory.",
          ],
          bullets: [
            "A trailing slash (logs/) matches directories only, preventing accidental exclusion of a regular file named logs.",
            "Double-star globs (**/foo, a/**/b) match zero or more nested directory levels.",
          ],
          codeExamples: [
            {
              title: "Re-including a nested file after ignoring its parent folder",
              language: "gitignore",
              code: "# Wrong: Git skips descending into build/, so !build/keep.txt never runs\nbuild/\n!build/keep.txt\n\n# Correct: ignore files inside build/* while keeping directory traversal open\nbuild/*\n!build/keep.txt",
            },
          ],
        },
        {
          heading: "Negation (!) edge cases and tracked-file behavior",
          paragraphs: [
            "The single most common .gitignore mistake is trying to re-include a file inside an ignored directory: once Git ignores a parent folder (dist/), it never traverses into it, so !dist/manifest.json has no effect unless you ignore dist/* instead. Additionally, .gitignore only affects untracked files; files already committed to the index stay tracked until removed with git rm --cached.",
          ],
        },
      ],
      howToUseSteps: [
        "Paste your .gitignore or .dockerignore rule lines, including comments (#), anchored slashes (/), and negation rules (!).",
        "Enter candidate relative repository file paths (one per line), such as src/index.ts, dist/app.js, or .env.local.",
        "Run the matcher to see which paths are ignored, which are whitelisted by negation rules, and which specific rule matched last.",
        "Refine directory trailing slashes and parent-folder exclusions until tracked files remain unignored.",
      ],
