    "css-specificity-calculator": {
      name: "CSS Specificity Calculator & Inspector",
      metadataTitle: "CSS Specificity Calculator & Inspector Online",
      description: "Calculate selector specificity tuples (IDs, classes, elements) and compare cascade overrides.",
      longDescription: "Compute CSS selector specificity tuples (A, B, C) for IDs, classes/attributes/pseudo-classes, and type/pseudo-elements according to W3C Selectors Level 4, including :where(), :is(), and :not() rules.",
      keywords: [
        "css specificity calculator",
        "css selector weight inspector",
        "specificity tuple calculator",
        "css :where :is specificity",
        "css cascade override checker",
        "selectors level 4 specificity",
        "debug css specificity",
      ],
      faqs: [
        {
          question: "Does repeating a class selector like .btn.btn increase its specificity?",
          answer: "Yes. Each simple selector in a compound selector is counted separately, so .btn.btn scores (0, 2, 0) and beats a single .btn at (0, 1, 0)—a classic hack to bump specificity without resorting to !important.",
        },
        {
          question: "Why does [id=\"header\"] have lower specificity than #header?",
          answer: "Attribute selectors always count in column B (0, 1, 0), even when targeting the id attribute, whereas the #header ID syntax counts in column A (1, 0, 0).",
        },
        {
          question: "Does an ID selector in #a #b still beat 15 stacked classes?",
          answer: "Yes. Modern browsers store each column separately and never carry column B into column A, so (1, 0, 0) always beats (0, 15, 0) or (0, 255, 0).",
        },
      ],
      answerSections: [
        {
          heading: "Selectors Level 4 specificity weights (A, B, C)",
          paragraphs: [
            "CSS specificity is a three-column tuple (A, B, C) compared lexicographically from left to right—no number of classes in column B can ever outrank a single ID selector in column A. Column A counts #id selectors, column B counts .class selectors, [attribute] selectors, and :pseudo-classes (:hover, :nth-child()), and column C counts type selectors (div, a) and ::pseudo-elements (::before).",
          ],
          bullets: [
            "Universal selector (*), combinators (+, >, ~, space), and :where(...) contribute (0, 0, 0) specificity.",
            ":is(...) and :not(...) take the specificity of their most specific argument inside the parentheses—not the sum of all arguments.",
          ],
          codeExamples: [
            {
              title: "Specificity comparison with :is() vs :where()",
              language: "CSS",
              code: "/* Specificity (1, 1, 1): #app (A=1) + .btn (B=1) + span (C=1) */\n#app .btn > span { color: navy; }\n\n/* Specificity (0, 2, 1): :is() adopts #id? No, max(#card, .panel) = (1,0,0)! */\n/* Using :where(#card, .panel) keeps weight at (0, 1, 1) with .title */\n:where(#card, .panel) .title h2 { color: slateblue; }",
            },
          ],
        },
        {
          heading: "Cascade Layers (@layer), inline styles, and !important",
          paragraphs: [
            "Specificity only breaks ties within the same cascade origin and layer. Under CSS Cascade Level 5, unlayered rules beat all @layer rules regardless of selector specificity, and earlier @layer declarations lose to later @layer declarations before specificity is even compared. Inline style=\"...\" attributes override author selector rules, while !important reverses normal layer and origin order.",
          ],
        },
      ],
      howToUseSteps: [
        "Enter one or more CSS selectors (such as #nav .menu > li.active::before or :is(article, section) h2) into the input box.",
        "Inspect the calculated (IDs, Classes/Attributes, Elements) specificity tuple for each selector.",
        "Compare two competing selectors side by side to determine which rule wins the CSS cascade.",
        "Refactor high-specificity ID chains using :where() or lower-weight class selectors to keep stylesheets maintainable.",
      ],
    },
