    "percentage-growth-calculator": {
      name: "Percentage Growth & Change Calculator",
      metadataTitle: "Percentage Growth & Change Calculator Online",
      description: "Calculate percentage increases, decreases, and compound metrics for dashboards.",
      longDescription: "Calculate exact percentage change, period-over-period growth rates, compound annual growth rate (CAGR), and reverse percentage baselines for SaaS KPIs, telemetry metrics, and SLO dashboards.",
      keywords: [
        "percentage growth calculator",
        "percentage change calculator",
        "cagr growth rate calculator",
        "saas metric growth formula",
        "percentage points vs percent",
        "kpi delta calculator",
        "reverse percentage calculator",
      ],
      faqs: [
        {
          question: "How do I reverse a percentage markup (e.g. find pre-tax price from a +20% total)?",
          answer: "Divide the final amount by (1 + rate), NOT subtract 20%: for a $120 total including +20%, the original price is $120 / 1.20 = $100 (whereas subtracting 20% would wrongly give $96).",
        },
        {
          question: "When is Compound Monthly Growth Rate (CMGR) better than arithmetic average growth?",
          answer: "Always when periods compound sequentially: if revenue grows +100% in month 1 and drops −50% in month 2, arithmetic average falsely claims +25% growth, while geometric CMGR correctly shows 0% net growth.",
        },
        {
          question: "How does Basis Points (bps) relate to percentage changes?",
          answer: "100 basis points equal 1 percentage point (1 bp = 0.01%), commonly used in latency SLAs, error budgets, and finance to avoid ambiguity between relative % and percentage points.",
        },
      ],
      answerSections: [
        {
          heading: "Percentage change, CAGR, and percentage-point pitfalls",
          paragraphs: [
            "Relative percentage change measures proportional growth from an initial baseline V₀ to a final value V₁: ((V₁ − V₀) / |V₀|) × 100%. Across N uniform time intervals (months, quarters, years), Compound Growth Rate (CAGR) equals ((V₁ / V₀)^(1/N) − 1) × 100%, smoothing out intermediate volatility into a steady per-period rate.",
          ],
          bullets: [
            "Asymmetry rule: a 50% drop followed by a 50% gain does NOT return to 100%—100 → 50 → 75 is still down 25%; recovering from a −50% drop requires a +100% gain.",
            "Distinguish relative percent from percentage points (pp): conversion rising from 2.0% to 3.0% is a +1.0 percentage point increase, which equals +50% relative growth.",
          ],
          codeExamples: [
            {
              title: "Safe percentage change and CAGR calculation in TypeScript",
              language: "TypeScript",
              code: "function growthMetrics(initial: number, current: number, periods = 1) {\n  if (initial === 0) return { pctChange: null, cagr: null };\n  const pctChange = ((current - initial) / Math.abs(initial)) * 100;\n  const cagr = initial > 0 && current > 0 && periods > 0\n    ? (Math.pow(current / initial, 1 / periods) - 1) * 100\n    : null;\n  return { delta: current - initial, pctChange, cagr };\n}",
            },
          ],
        },
        {
          heading: "Zero and negative baseline edge cases in dashboards",
          paragraphs: [
            "When the baseline V₀ is zero (e.g., 0 errors last week, 4 errors today), percentage change is mathematically undefined (division by zero)—dashboards should display \"N/A\" or \"+4 (from 0)\" rather than Infinity%. When V₀ is negative (such as net profit swinging from −$10k to +$5k), dividing by |V₀| preserves the intuitive positive sign (+150% improvement), whereas dividing by raw signed V₀ would misleadingly report −150%.",
          ],
        },
      ],
      howToUseSteps: [
        "Enter the baseline (Initial / Previous) value and the new (Current / Target) metric value.",
        "Read the absolute delta (New − Old) and relative percentage change ((New − Old) / |Old| × 100%).",
        "Optionally enter the number of periods N to compute Compound Growth Rate per period: (New / Old)^(1/N) − 1.",
        "Check percentage-point differences vs relative percentage growth when reporting conversion-rate shifts.",
      ],
    },
