      faqs: [
        {
          question: "Does JavaScript have atomic groups or possessive quantifiers to stop ReDoS?",
          answer: "No—ECMAScript RegExp lacks (?>...) and *+. In JavaScript, eliminate overlapping groups, limit input length before matching, or use Google’s linear-time RE2 binding (re2 on npm).",
        },
        {
          question: "Why can .* inside a pattern like /.*a.*b.*/ cause polynomial O(N^2) or O(N^3) slowdowns?",
          answer: "Each greedy .* consumes the entire string and backtracks character-by-character; chaining k such wildcards forces O(N^k) combinations when the final token is missing. Replace .* with negated character classes like [^a]* when possible.",
        },
        {
          question: "Does the sticky (y) or anchored (^) flag reduce backtracking risk?",
          answer: "Anchoring with ^ prevents the engine from retrying an expensive sub-pattern at every starting offset O(N) times, cutting polynomial slowdowns—though nested quantifiers inside ^(...)+$ remain exponential.",
        },
      ],
      answerSections: [
        {
          heading: "How catastrophic backtracking (ReDoS) freezes NFA engines",
          paragraphs: [
            "Backtracking NFA regex engines (used by JavaScript V8, Python re, PCRE, and older Java) explore every possible way to partition an input string until a full match succeeds or all paths fail. When a pattern contains nested quantifiers like (a+)+ or overlapping alternations like (a|ab)+ followed by a failing suffix, an input of length N triggers O(2^N) backtracking steps—freezing Node.js event loops for minutes on just 30 characters.",
          ],
          bullets: [
            "Eliminate nested quantifiers: rewrite ^(a+)+$ to ^a+$ or use mutually exclusive character classes.",
            "In PCRE/Java/Python 3.11+, atomic groups (?>...) and possessive quantifiers (++ / *+) lock matched substrings and discard backtracking states.",
          ],
          codeExamples: [
            {
              title: "Vulnerable email-like regex vs. linear-time rewrite",
              language: "javascript",
              code: "// Vulnerable O(2^n) on \"aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa!\"\nconst vulnerable = /^([a-zA-Z0-9]+[-._]?)+@[a-zA-Z0-9.-]+$/;\n\n// Linear O(n): enforce separator explicitly so sub-groups cannot overlap\nconst linear = /^[a-zA-Z0-9]+(?:[-._][a-zA-Z0-9]+)*@[a-zA-Z0-9.-]+$/;",
            },
          ],
        },
        {
          heading: "Why failing inputs trigger worst-case exponential latency",
          paragraphs: [
            "Benchmarking a regex only against valid examples hides ReDoS vulnerabilities because a valid match succeeds immediately on the engine’s first greedy pass. Catastrophic backtracking erupts when the prefix matches dozens of characters and only the final anchor or delimiter fails—forcing the engine to retry every combinatorial split of the prefix before returning false.",
          ],
        },
      ],
      howToUseSteps: [
        "Enter your regular expression pattern and flags (such as i, m, or s).",
        "Provide a sample or adversarial failing test string (e.g., repeated characters ending with a mismatch).",
        "Inspect detected ReDoS anti-patterns such as (a+)+, ([a-z]+)*, or overlapping alternation branches.",
        "Compare step counts before and after rewriting the regex using non-backtracking or linear alternatives.",
      ],
